END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events": "https://events.yorku.ca/events/york-circle-lecture-series-8/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events
SUMMARY:York Circle Lecture Series
DTSTART:20261114T140000Z
DTEND:20261114T180000Z
DTSTAMP:20261020T161300Z
UID:MEC-735f505f81a75048db0947fe995b58e9@events.yorku.ca
RECURRENCE-ID:20261114T140000Z
SEQUENCE:6
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://events.yorku.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/
 09/YorkCircle.jpg
CATEGORIES:Advancement/Alumni,Lassonde School of Engineering
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20261020
DESCRIPTION:Don’t miss out on the chance to hear from some of York’s l
 eading faculty members on a wide range of fascinating topics at our next Y
 ork Circle session. Connect with other alumni\, gain valuable insights fro
 m our featured professors\, and ask your own questions on important topics
 .\nDate: Saturday\, Nov. 14\nTime: 9 a.m. – 1 p.m.\nLocation: Life Scie
 nces Building\, Keele Campus\nTickets: $5 includes coffee\, light snacks a
 nd lunch.\n\nAttendees will have the opportunity to choose one lecture fr
 om each session during the registration process.\nFirst sessions – 10 a
 .m. \n\nDiagnostic and Prognostic Medical Devices: From research to real-
 world impact\nPresented by Neda Salahandish\, Assistant Professor\, Electr
 ical Engineering and Computer Science and Biomedical Engineering\, Lassond
 e School of Engineering\, and CEO\, WearDOXX\nDrawing on examples from her
  research\, Dr. Salahandish will discuss the development of diagnostic and
  prognostic medical devices\, including electrochemical biosensors\, micro
 fluidic point-of-care technologies\, wearable devices\, and AI-enabled hea
 lth platforms.\n\nAI at the Dentist’s Chair: Transforming periodontal di
 sease detection with DePERIO\nPresented by Ebrahim Ghafar-Zadeh\, Associat
 e Professor\, Department of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science\nG
 um disease is one of the most common oral health conditions\, yet early de
 tection can be challenging and often depends on specialized clinical asses
 sment. This talk will introduce DePERIO\, an AI-enabled portable technolog
 y developed to support rapid and accessible assessment of periodontal heal
 th.\n\nSecond Sessions – 11:20 a.m.\n\nMicrochips Meet Mind: Tiny electr
 onics that listen to\, and heal\, the brain\nPresented by Hossein Kassiri\
 , Associate Professor\, Department of Electrical Engineering and Computer 
 Science\, Lassonde School of Engineering\nProfessor Hossein Kassiri will s
 hare how his lab will bring hospital-quality brain monitoring into a comfo
 rtable headband through a tiny\, wireless devices that detects epileptic s
 eizures and stops them before they spread. This innovation could one day r
 estore sight to people with retinal diseases.\n\nFrom Lab to Life: Multifu
 nctional materials and systems that sense\, adapt and perform\nPresented b
 y Garrett Melenka\, Associate Professor\, Department of Mechanical Enginee
 ring\, Lassonde School of Engineering and Gerd Grau\, Associate Professor\
 , Department of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science\, Lassonde Sch
 ool of Engineering\nBy combining expertise in composite materials and addi
 tive manufacturing with printed electronics and sensing systems\, Professo
 rs Garrett Melenka and Gerd Grau have collaborated on a number of projects
  from multifunctional carbon fiber composites to wearable smart knee brace
  technology. These talks bring the two researchers together to discuss how
  multifunctional materials move from the lab bench to practical\, real-wor
 ld devices.\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261105
LOCATION:Life Sciences Building
ORGANIZER;CN="Office of Alumni Engagement":MAILTO:alumni@yorku.ca
PRIORITY:5
TRANSP:OPAQUE
URL:https://events.yorku.ca/events/york-circle-lecture-series-8/
X-SOURCE:York University
END:VEVENT
