DTSTART:20261116T200000Z
DTEND:20261116T210000Z
DTSTAMP:20261114T061722Z
UID:j9eircrdr57uumrqbaia3a7obs@google.com
CREATED:20260917T142125Z
DESCRIPTION:<p>Title: <b>The Fate of Neutron Star Mergers</b></p>\n\n<p>Abs
 tract: In this talk\, I will discuss the outcome of binary neutron star mer
 gers and its consequences for their multi-messenger emission and nucleosynt
 hesis yields. At one extreme\, neutron star mergers can undergo prompt coll
 apse to a black hole\, which produces only faint electromagnetic emission. 
 The threshold mass for this to occur is sensitive to how compressible matte
 r is at the highest densities. Lower-mass systems can produce short or long
 -lived remnants\, which evolve due to the presence of hydrodynamic and magn
 etohydrodynamic instabilities. I will argue that these mergers are accompan
 ied by the ejection of substantial amount of material and can explain the k
 ilonova seen in AT2017gfo. I will also discuss what postmerger gravitationa
 l waves can tell us about thermal effects and QCD phase transitions\, and h
 ow the size of the disk may decide whether a merger produces a short or a l
 ong gamma-ray burst.</p>\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261113T184904Z
LOCATION:1318A
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:David Radice (Penn State University)
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
