BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events": "https://events.yorku.ca/events/tubman-talks-with-dr-marie-laure-belomo-onguene-peak-technology-banking-and-gender-economic-inclusion-in-africatubman-talks-with-dr-marie-laure-belomo-onguene-peak-technology-banking-and-gender/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events
SUMMARY:Tubman Talks with Marie Laure Belomo Onguene: Peak Technology\, Ba
 nking and Gender Economic Inclusion in Africa
DTSTART:20261119T190000Z
DTEND:20261119T203000Z
DTSTAMP:20261109T182100Z
UID:MEC-16e289983cce14eda9b2ade90076c44d@events.yorku.ca
RECURRENCE-ID:20261119T190000Z
SEQUENCE:3
CATEGORIES:ORUs
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20261109
DESCRIPTION:Tubman Talks with Marie Laure Belomo Onguene: Peak Technology\
 , Banking and Gender Economic Inclusion in Africa\nDate: Thursday\, Novemb
 er 19\, 2026\nTime: 2:30pm – 4:00pm EST\nLocation: Zoom\nRegister Here:
  https://yorku.zoom.us/meeting/register/K-bk-9K5Qm-JZMX3T3pK8g\nTitle: Pea
 k Technology\, Banking and Gender Economic Inclusion in Africa\nAbstract\n
 The focus of the study is how mobile money moderates the effect of peak te
 chnology on gender economic inclusion in Africa\, with thresholds of mobil
 e money at which the effect\nof peak technology on gender economic inclusi
 on is mitigated\, as well as evidence of positive synergies between digita
 l finance and frontier technologies. Using a panel dataset of 31 African c
 ountries over the period 2010-2023\, and drawing on fixed-effects models\,
  Driscoll–Kraay robust estimations\, Lewbel (2012) instrumental variable
  approaches\, and quantile regressions\, the study produces the following 
 key findings. First\, the baseline linear estimates indicate that frontier
  technology readiness (FRTI) is\, on average\, positively and significantl
 y associated with the share of women in the labor force. Second\, interact
 ive and instrumental variable specifications reveal a fundamental structur
 al heterogeneity: when mobile money and digital payment services are absen
 t\, the marginal effect of FRTI on women’s economic inclusion turns nega
 tive or insignificant. Third\, threshold analysis identifies specific leve
 ls of financial digitalization beyond which the adverse effect of frontier
  technologies is fully mitigated or reversed into a positive synergy. Thes
 e thresholds vary across specifications (ranging from approximately 0.53 t
 o 1.65 on the digital finance scales) but consistently confirm a non-linea
 r complementarity between technological capability and digital financial i
 nclusion. Fourth\, quantile regression results establish that the inclusiv
 e effect of technology is most pronounced at the upper end of the distribu
 tion of female labor force participation. Policy implications are discusse
 d.\nBio\nMarie Laure BELOMO ONGUENE is a lecturer in the Faculty of Econom
 ics and Management at the University of Yaoundé 2 Soa. She holds a Ph.D. 
 from the University of Dschang in Cameroon and served as a postdoctoral re
 searcher in the Open AIR program at the Center for Law\, Technology\, and 
 Society at the University of Ottawa. In addition\, she serves as a visitin
 g researcher in the Department of Economics at York University in Canada u
 nder the supervision of Pralewa Yinka Olawoye. Between May 2023 and August
  2023\, she received a research mobility grant from the Observatory of the
  Economic Francophonie at the University of Lomé in Togo. Her research fo
 cuses on the socioeconomic challenges that emigration poses to migrants’
  countries of origin. She is particularly interested in economic complexit
 y\, women’s political empowerment\, and food security.\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261113
LOCATION:(3rd flr York Lanes\, Rm 314)
ORGANIZER;CN="The Harriet Tubman Institute":MAILTO:tubman@yorku.ca
PRIORITY:5
TRANSP:OPAQUE
URL:https://events.yorku.ca/events/tubman-talks-with-dr-marie-laure-belomo
 -onguene-peak-technology-banking-and-gender-economic-inclusion-in-africatu
 bman-talks-with-dr-marie-laure-belomo-onguene-peak-technology-banking-and-
 gender/
X-SOURCE:York University
END:VEVENT
