X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events": "https://events.yorku.ca/events/tubman-talks-roundtable-decolonizing-knowledge-production-in-ituri-north-and-south-kivu-provinces-of-the-democratic-republic-of-congo-extractivism-environmental-governance-gender-justice-and-com/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events
SUMMARY:Tubman Talks Roundtable: Decolonizing Knowledge Production in Itur
 i\, North and South Kivu Provinces of the Democratic Republic of Congo: Ex
 tractivism\, Environmental Governance\, Gender Justice\, and Community Kno
 wledge
DTSTART:20261126T190000Z
DTEND:20261126T203000Z
DTSTAMP:20261112T164000Z
UID:MEC-db07748a120b8752dcac49ddfe281b71@events.yorku.ca
RECURRENCE-ID:20261126T190000Z
SEQUENCE:2
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://events.yorku.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/1
 1/Screenshot-2026-11-11-151927.png
CATEGORIES:ORUs
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20261112
DESCRIPTION:Date: Thursday\, November 26\, 2026\nTime: 2:00-3:30pm EST\nLo
 cation: Virtual\nRegister here: https://yorku.zoom.us/meeting/register/z7
 joamFfT3aKIAIwCsVaEw\nTitle: Decolonizing Knowledge Production in Ituri\, 
 North and South Kivu Provinces of the Democratic Republic of Congo: Extrac
 tivism\, Environmental Governance\, Gender Justice\, and Community Knowled
 ge\nAbstract\nThe provinces of Ituri\, North and South Kivu in the Democra
 tic Republic of Congo (DRC) are shaped by complex intersections of resourc
 e extraction\, environmental governance\, armed conflict\, gendered violen
 ce\, and community resistance. Yet dominant institutional and external fra
 meworks often produce knowledge about these realities in ways that margina
 lize local experiences\, Indigenous and community-based knowledge\, and th
 e intellectual contributions of Congolese women. This presentation examine
 s the possibilities and challenges of decolonizing knowledge production by
  centering locally grounded research\, lived experiences\, and community p
 erspectives in understanding the political\, environmental\, and gendered 
 dimensions of conflict in eastern DRC.\nBringing together complementary pe
 rspectives on extractivism and resource-related dynamics in Ituri\, enviro
 nmental governance and women’s agency in North Kivu\, and gender justice
 \, sexual and gender-based violence (SGBV)\, as well as peacebuilding in t
 he Kivus\, the presentation explores how dominant knowledge systems shape 
 understandings of conflict and whose voices are recognized as legitimate s
 ources of knowledge. It further considers how community-based research\, f
 eminist and decolonial approaches\, and survivor-centered practices can ch
 allenge extractive research relationships and support more accountable\, i
 nclusive\, and transformative forms of knowledge production.\nRather than 
 positioning Congolese communities only as research subjects or victims of 
 conflict\, the discussion recognizes them as knowledge holders\, analysts\
 , and active participants in shaping responses to violence\, environmental
  harm\, and social injustice. The session will conclude with an extended c
 ommunity dialogue\, inviting participants to reflect on research ethics\, 
 representation\, power\, and the possibilities of building more reciprocal
  relationships between universities\, researchers\, and communities in the
  DRC and its diaspora.\nThe session will include 45 minutes of speaker pre
 sentations\, followed by 45 minutes of community discussion and exchange. 
 Creating space for collective reflection\, the event aims to foster dialog
 ue on how knowledge can help us understand the realities of eastern DRC an
 d advance gender justice\, environmental accountability\, and locally grou
 nded peacebuilding.\nKeywords: Decolonizing knowledge\; eastern Democratic
  Republic of Congo\; Ituri\; North and South Kivu\; extractivism\; environ
 mental governance\; feminist research\; SGBV\; community knowledge\; peace
 building.\n \nSpeakers: Rose Ukeci Uwodha\, Borauzima Feza Esther and Kas
 horo Nyenyezi (All Congolese Scholars)\nBio of Presenters\nRose Ukeci Uwod
 ha is a PhD candidate in sociology and anthropology at the University of M
 ons (Belgium). She will speak about Who Found the Gold? Extractivism and C
 ommunity Knowledge in Kilo-Moto\, Ituri. Her doctoral research examines di
 scourse\, power and resistance in the gold mining territory of Kilo-Moto\,
  in north-eastern Democratic Republic of Congo. She studies how religious 
 movements\, artisanal miners\, customary authorities and former combatants
  contest or reshapes the extractive order. Her work starts from the offici
 al history of the mine\, which dates the “discovery” of gold to 1903\,
  and the arrival of prospectors sent by the Congo Free State. This narrati
 ve erased the knowledge of the Ukebu people\, who knew and worked the meta
 l long before Europeans arrived\, and it still shapes how the state\, mini
 ng companies and donors speak about the mine. Against this background\, sh
 e shows that the knowledge of local communities is political. For some rel
 igious movements\, gold is alive and certain places are sacred. Artisanal 
 miners create their own rules and techniques\, and women miners organize i
 n cooperatives to defend their place in a male-dominated sector. Her resea
 rch also shows that “the local” is not homogeneous\, since some custom
 ary authorities adopt the language of good governance to capture mining re
 venues. Taking community knowledge seriously without romanticizing it is a
 t the heart of her approach.\n \nBora will be talking about Producing Kno
 wledge with Women Working in and around Virunga National Park: A Global So
 uth Researcher’s Perspective on Armed Conflict Dynamics in Eastern DRC (
 Dissertation title?)\nBorauzima Feza Esther is a Congolese (DRC) researche
 r with a Master’s degree in Political Science and a Specialized Master
 ’s degree in Gender Studies. She teaches at the Official University of B
 ukavu and is a researcher with the Research Group on Conflict and Human Se
 curity. A PhD candidate in Sociology and Anthropology at the University of
  Mons\, she conducts research on armed conflicts\, gender\, and natural re
 source governance around Virunga National Park in eastern DRC.\nHer work e
 xamines how female agency is exercised or constrained in contexts of extre
 me precarity and vulnerability. In particular\, she investigates the traje
 ctories of women combatants involved in caregiving and the mystical-milita
 ry practices of armed groups\, former female combatants imprisoned on espi
 onage charges\, and women reselling forest resources in collaboration with
  armed groups. Her research draws on Afro-feminist\, decolonial\, and crit
 ical approaches. She also engages critically with the production of knowle
 dge\, North–South relations\, and the ethical challenges of conducting r
 esearch in sensitive contexts.\n \nDr. Kashoro’s contribution will focu
 s on how sexual and gender-based violence (SGBV)\, women’s experiences\,
  and community knowledge can help us decolonize research and advance peace
 building in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC).\nKashoro Nyenyezi is a
  scholar and community-based researcher whose work focuses on social justi
 ce\, sexual and gender-based violence\, feminist research\, and peacebuild
 ing in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC) and its diaspora. She ho
 lds a PhD in Social Justice Education from the Ontario Institute for Studi
 es in Education (OISE)\, University of Toronto\, and is an independent res
 earch fellow at York University’s Harriet Tubman Institute. Her research
  examines the intersections of land\, gendered violence\, power\, and colo
 nial legacies\, with particular attention to the experiences and knowledge
  of women in conflict-affected communities. Through her research and commu
 nity engagement with Helping Hand for Survivors\, she advances survivor-ce
 ntred\, feminist\, and decolonial approaches to understanding sexual and g
 ender-based violence (SGBV)\, promoting women’s agency\, and strengtheni
 ng community-led peacebuilding. Her work challenges approaches that positi
 on Congolese women and communities merely as subjects of research\, instea
 d recognizing them as knowledge producers whose experiences and perspectiv
 es are essential to understanding violence\, advancing gender justice\, an
 d building sustainable peace. For this presentation\, she brings an interd
 isciplinary perspective on how research can become more accountable to the
  communities whose lives and struggles it seeks to understand.\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261112
ORGANIZER;CN="The Harriet Tubman Institute":MAILTO:tubman@yorku.ca
PRIORITY:5
