BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Torontobotanicalgarden": "https://torontobotanicalgarden.ca/event/author-talk-steven-biggs/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:torontobotanicalgarden
X-SOURCE:Toronto Botanical Garden
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261128T113000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261128T130000
DTSTAMP:20261114T011902
CREATED:20261111T200517Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261111T200528Z
UID:10002050-1795865400-1795870800@torontobotanicalgarden.ca
SUMMARY:Author Talk: Steven Biggs
DESCRIPTION:Grow Figs Where You Think You Can’t: Stories\, Ingenuity\, and Homegrown Figs\nWhat makes someone build a “figatorium\,” collect hundreds of varieties\, or tuck fig trees into bed for a Canadian winter? \n \nJoin Toronto horticulturist and author Steven Biggs for a mix of storytelling and practical fig-growing advice celebrating the revised and expanded edition of Grow Figs Where You Think You Can’t. \n \nMeet some of the fascinating growers featured in the book—from a generous mentor in an Ontario backyard to a California fig hunter—and discover the family traditions\, curiosity\, and ingenuity behind their gardens. Along the way\, Steven shares ideas for growing figs here in the Toronto area\, getting ripe fruit\, and finding room for a little Mediterranean flavour at home. \n \nWhether you already grow figs or are simply curious\, come for the stories\, bring your questions\, and stay for the book signing. \n \nSaturday\, November 28\, 11:30 a.m. to 1 p.m.\nFree to attend. Please register beforehand so we know you’re coming!\n \nAbout Steven Biggs\nRecognized by Garden Making magazine as one of the “Green Gang” of Canadians making a difference in horticulture\, Steven Biggs is a Toronto-based horticulturist\, author\, and host of the award-winning Food Garden Life Show. A former horticulture instructor with George Brown College and Durham College\, he has spent decades helping gardeners grow edible plants in unlikely places. In his own Toronto garden\, his experiments have included driveway straw-bale gardens\, a rooftop kitchen garden\, wicking beds\, fruit plantings\, an edible-themed front yard\, and a fig collection that got slightly out of hand.  \n \nSteven’s fascination with figs took off in 1993\, when he spent a summer working at a nursery with the UK National Collection of figs. Since 2009\, he has been teaching\, writing\, speaking\, and interviewing growers about figs in cold climates. His first fig book\, Grow Figs Where You Think You Can’t\, won a Silver Award of Achievement from the Garden Writers Association. Steven’s fig writing has appeared in Fine Gardening\, Garden Making\, The Gardener\, and Edible Toronto.  \n \nSteven continues the fig conversation through his Fig Culture podcast and newsletter\, where he shares stories\, interviews\, and practical ideas for growing figs in cold climates and containers. Find his fig tips and other food-gardening ideas at foodgardenlife.com. \n \nJoin Toronto Botanical Garden\n \nJoin the Toronto Botanical Garden (TBG) as a member to get a discount on this program\, upcoming events and classes\, and free parking. A TBG membership helps you deepen your knowledge and connection to the natural world\, become a part of a dynamic community of plant people\, and be inspired! Become a member!\n \nAdditional Program Information\n\n\n\nPlease review our Refund Policy and Terms of Agreement.\nFor more information on accessibility at the garden please review our Accessibility Features webpage. If you have any accessibility requirements during your program please email programsupport@torontobotanicalgarden.ca.\nTo learn more about the Toronto Botanical Garden and our programs\, please review our list of Frequently Asked Questions.
URL:https://torontobotanicalgarden.ca/event/author-talk-steven-biggs/
LOCATION:Toronto Botanical Garden\, 777 Lawrence Avenue East\, Toronto\, ON\, M3C 1P2\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Gardening
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://torontobotanicalgarden.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/bdf40e5d8cde4da41537d8930c70f10f.jpg
END:VEVENT
