BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Meetup Toronto Ai Machine Learning Data Science": "https://www.meetup.com/toronto-ai-machine-learning-data-science/events/317415243/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:meetup_toronto_ai_machine_learning_data_science
X-SOURCE:Meetup: Toronto AI & ML
UID:event_317415243@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20261114T061844Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T140000
SUMMARY:Dec 8 - How to Let Long-tail Guide Your 3D Annotation Workshop
DESCRIPTION:Toronto AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup\nJoin
  us on Dec 8 for the “How to Let Long-tail Guide Your 3D Annotation” w
 orkshop with [Eric Hofesmann](https://www.linkedin.com/in/eric-hofesmann/)
 .\n\n**Date\, Time and Location**\n\nDec 08\, 2026\n9:00 AM - 10:00 AM PST
 \nOnline. **[Register for the Zoom!](https://voxel51.com/events/how-to-let
 -long-tail-guide-your-3d-annotation-december-8-2026)**\n\nAutonomous drivi
 ng models rarely fail on the common case. They fail on the long-tail: the 
 pedestrian stepping out from behind a parked truck or a construction zone 
 that matches nothing in the training set. Each of those moments lives in s
 ynchronized multi-sensor data\, made up of a LiDAR sweep\, a ring of camer
 as\, a timestamp\, and a calibration. Fixing one means finding it\, labeli
 ng it in 3D\, and proving the next model handles it.\n\nIn this live sessi
 on\, we will take long-tail driving scenarios from discovery to 3D labels 
 to evaluation\, and make the case that 3D ground truth should be built whe
 re your data lives\, not exported away from it.
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/toronto-ai-machine-learning-data-scie
 nce/events/317415243/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20261113T194807Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261113T194807Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Philosophy": "https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/logic-and-philosophy-of-science-group-gordon-belot-michigan/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:philosophy
X-SOURCE:UofT Philosophy
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T150000
DTSTAMP:20261109T153615Z
CREATED:20260910T180221Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261109T153615Z
UID:35768-1796731200-1796742000@philosophy.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Logic and Philosophy of Science Group (Gordon Belot\, Michigan)
DESCRIPTION:The Logic and Philosophy of Science Research Group is pleased to welcome as its guest speaker Gordon Belot\, the Lawrence Sklar Collegiate Professor of Philosophy at the University of Michigan. Professor Belot’s primary interests are in philosophy of physics\, philosophy of science\, metaphysics\, and epistemology. His most recent book\, Accelerating Expansion: Philosophy and Physics with a Positive Cosmological Constant\, explores some of the philosophical consequences of modern cosmology\, focusing on the significance of the discovery of the accelerating expansion of the universe for our understanding of time\, geometry\, and physics. \nTalk Title\nComputable Epistemology—An Idiosyncratic Primer \nTalk Abstract\nSome morals. (a) In some settings\, there is no such thing as an optimal method for learning (so someone who tells you there is a unique rational response to any body of evidence may be telling you something strange). (b) In some settings\, induction-unfriendly scenarios are common (and do not require demonic fine-tuning). (c) In some settings\, computable learners pay a price for following intuitively reasonable strategies (like relying on the simplest hypothesis consistent with their data). (d) In some settings\, computable learners pay no price for being Bayesian—but only if they are willing to use priors inconsistent with their background knowledge.
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/logic-and-philosophy-of-science-group-gordon-belot-michigan/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building\, Room 418\, 170 St. George Street\, Toronto\, Ontario\, M5R 2M8\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Graduate,St. George
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/Belot-new-resized.png
END:VEVENT
